# Extra torrc lines for the relay's hidden service — the `tor-onion` service,
# behind `docker compose --profile onion`.
#
# Copy this file, uncomment what you want, and point ONION_EXTRA_LOCAL at your
# copy; compose mounts it at /etc/tor/onion.extra.conf and the entrypoint
# appends it to the generated torrc, last, so it wins on any option tor takes
# once. A file of nothing but comments (this one, as shipped) is skipped
# entirely — the log line only appears when something was actually appended.
#
# Everything here is off by default on purpose: each one trades something an
# operator has to decide about, and two of the three cannot be undone on the
# same key.

# --- Latency -----------------------------------------------------------------
# A hidden service's default path is six hops — three the client builds, three
# the service builds. Single-hop mode drops the service's three to one, which
# roughly halves round-trip latency, and for a relay whose clearnet address is
# already published there is little left to hide: the trade is that the
# rendezvous point learns this server's IP.
#
# ONE WAY, on a given key. Tor's own warning: "If the
# HiddenServiceNonAnonymousMode option is changed, Tor will refuse to launch
# hidden services from the same directories". Turning it back off means a new
# key, which means a new .onion address and every client that had the old one
# pointed at nothing. Decide before you publish the address, not after.
#
# Both lines are required together — tor refuses to start with SingleHopMode
# alone ("does not provide any server anonymity"). The bundled torrc's
# `SocksPort 0` is the other precondition, and it is already there.
#
# HiddenServiceNonAnonymousMode 1
# HiddenServiceSingleHopMode 1

# --- Introduction flooding ---------------------------------------------------
# Tor's onion-service proof-of-work defense (0.4.8+). Idle until the service is
# under an introduction flood, at which point clients that support it pay a
# small computed cost and get served ahead of the flood. Clients that do not
# support it still connect normally when there is no attack.
#
# HiddenServicePoWDefensesEnabled 1

# --- Reachability ------------------------------------------------------------
# How many introduction points the service maintains (default 3, max 20). More
# costs a little upkeep and survives more of them going away at once; worth
# raising for a busy relay, pointless for a quiet one.
#
# HiddenServiceNumIntroductionPoints 5
